{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966795,"nonce":"uQT78eyEnKBNtulJwGG6t5jcd1M=","title":"A Zyntax JIT rebuild dropped every loaded module's globals but one; fixed upstream, except","message":"RESOLVED upstream (zyntax `a73f4d0`): every loaded module is kept in\nload order and recompiled after a rebuild, and the op table is resolved\nat push time instead of read from the instance. All six repros pass.\n\nSTILL ORDER-DEPENDENT: `bind_fiber_handler_instance` resolves the table\nat bind time, and a machine binds once, when it is created.\n`fiber_bind_handler` copies that address into the fiber's saved handler\nsegment, which nothing re-resolves. So a machine minted before a later\nfile loads still dispatches through a stale table INSIDE A RESUME. That\nis why Blinc mints machines only at the end of a project compile: with\nper-file minting the render path is fine (`pg_nav` passes) and every\ntest that dispatches faults in `krio_fiber::fiber_run`.\n\nThe original defect, for context. Zyntax's\n`rebuild_with_accumulated_symbols` replaces the JIT module and clears\n`global_map`; `compile_module` then recompiles only `current_module`.\nBlinc triggers that rebuild once per file, because each file's `$view`\nsymbols must be exported and finalised before the next file can link\nagainst them.\n\nBoth orderings fail:\n\n- Instance created per file: `HandlerInstanceEntry.table` caches the\n  op-table address, and after the rebuild it points at string\n  constants. `__zyntax_effect_lookup_op` returns them, the perform site\n  sees non-null and calls it. SIGBUS at an address like\n  `0x6764695700000007` (`{7, \"Widg\"}`).\n- Instance created after the whole project: `try_handler_push_info`\n  reports \"`$optable$…` is in the module but was never declared to the\n  backend\". No frame, so the perform falls back to the static op, which\n  reads an implicit `self` from null. SIGSEGV at `ldr x0, [x0]`.\n\n`missing_stateful_handler` does not catch either: the reachability walk\nresolves callees with `module.functions.get(\u0026id)` inside one module, so\na cross-module call ends it and it answers `None`.\n\nWritten up at `zyntax/docs/A_MODULE_LOSES_ITS_GLOBALS_ON_REBUILD.md`\n(975728f). Repro: `cargo test -p blinc_dsl_core --test\nimported_module_fsm -- --ignored`. Blocks the four playground tests\n(`pg_nav`, `pg_trigger`, `pg_grow_cost`, `pg_reactive_baseline`).\n\nDebugging note: JIT frames have no symbols, and the crash address is\nwhatever data the table now overlays. What identified it was dumping\n`get_function_ptr` for every candidate name in the same process, then\nmatching `$lr` against the nearest preceding address. Related:\ngotcha unhandled effect crashes, project fsm as fiber,\nproject multi module blinc plan.","files":null}]}